# A COLLECTION BOUND FROM A LOCAL'S `.take` MOVES IT. The binding copied the
# collection's handle and left the local holding it, so both destroyed it at
# scope end: `ys: xs.take` on a ListRef, a ListVal, a MapVV or a SetVal was a
# use-after-free or a double free, at the top of a function and in an `if` arm
# alike. A bare move (`ys: xs`) and a field's take zeroed their source already.
run: function {c: bool} is {
    xs: ListRef String
    xs.append "item".string
    ys: xs.take
    vs: ListVal i64
    vs.append 4
    ws: vs.take
    m: MapVV k: i64 v: i64
    m.set key: 1 value: 2
    n: m.take
    s: SetVal k: i64
    s.add item: 3
    s2: s.take
    print "\{ys.length} \{ws.length} \{n.length} \{s2.length}"
    zs: ListRef String
    zs.append "arm".string
    if c then {
        taken: zs.take
        print "arm took \{taken.length}"
    }
}

main: function is {
    run c: true
    run c: false
}
